* Question: on how many hosts is the crypto stack even capable of negotiating a
* hybrid group?
*
* This is the search that decides whether a migration plan can be costed.
* Replacing a certificate on a host whose TLS library cannot do hybrid key
* agreement achieves nothing, so capability is the gate that comes before the
* certificate work, not after it.
*
* Thresholds are the ones the assessment pipeline resolves from primary
* sources; keep them in one lookup rather than in the search, so the SOC number
* and the assessment number cannot drift:
*   pqc_capability_thresholds.csv: product,min_version,source
*   openssl,3.5.0,https://openssl-library.org/news/openssl-3.5-notes/
*   nss,3.118,...
*
* Source: any inventory feed normalised to the CIM Certificates or Endpoint
* datamodel; Splunk_TA_nix package inventory works as well.
* Validation status: [inference] - not run against a live indexer.

index=inventory sourcetype=package_inventory earliest=-7d@d
| eval product=lower(package_name)
| search product IN ("openssl", "libssl", "nss", "libnss3", "gnutls", "libressl", "boringssl")
| lookup pqc_capability_thresholds.csv product OUTPUT min_version, source
| eval have=replace(package_version, "[^0-9.].*$", "")
| eval cmp_have=printf("%03d%03d%03d",
      tonumber(mvindex(split(have,"."),0)),
      coalesce(tonumber(mvindex(split(have,"."),1)),0),
      coalesce(tonumber(mvindex(split(have,"."),2)),0))
| eval cmp_min=printf("%03d%03d%03d",
      tonumber(mvindex(split(min_version,"."),0)),
      coalesce(tonumber(mvindex(split(min_version,"."),1)),0),
      coalesce(tonumber(mvindex(split(min_version,"."),2)),0))
| eval pqc_capability=case(
    isnull(min_version), "unknown",
    cmp_have >= cmp_min, "capable",
    1==1, "not_capable")
| stats dc(host) AS hosts BY product, pqc_capability
| eventstats sum(hosts) AS fleet
| eval pct=round(100*hosts/fleet, 1)
| sort product, pqc_capability

* Version comparison is done on a zero-padded numeric key rather than string
* comparison, because "3.5" sorts below "3.10" as a string and a truncated
* version string would otherwise be misclassified as not capable. That exact
* defect shipped once in the assessment pipeline (F2, sprint 6) and is worth
* not repeating in the query language.
*
* `unknown` is a reported outcome, not a silent drop: a product with no
* threshold in the lookup must show up as unresolved rather than disappear.
